/* =====================================================================
   CARTHAGE — set syllabus

   Rebuilt against the Content Bible (v1).

   Two rules from that document govern this file:

   §2  One screen, one idea, 80-180 words. The previous version of this
       Set ran to 288 words a screen, which the Bible calls a failed
       entry however accurate the sentences are. Every beat here is cut
       to a single development.

   §5  Where the Bible supplies prose (CAR-01 to CAR-16), its wording is
       used as written. Where it does not — the cothon, the constitution,
       the Tophet, the sources warning, Hanno's Atlantic voyage, the
       empire-of-harbours model — the app's own material is kept and cut
       to length. The Bible's own header says v1 covers only what the
       live campaign had reached, so silence there is not a deletion
       instruction.

   Battles are still deliberately thin here and live in the Wars tab, per
   the Bible's §1: the Set explains why a war begins and what changes,
   the War page does terrain, deployment and phases.
   ===================================================================== */

CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "carth-dido", set: "carthage", act: "Foundation", title: "The Legend of Queen Elissa", era: "Traditional · 814 BC", minutes: 5,
  intro: "A Phoenician princess, a murdered husband, and a bargain about an ox-hide. None of it is history — and it is still worth knowing, because of what the Romans did with it.",
  beats: [
    { title: "A woman running from her brother",
      text: ["The story starts in **Tyre**, on the coast of what is now Lebanon. **Elissa** — the Greeks called her **Dido** — is the king's daughter. Her brother **Pygmalion** takes the throne and murders her husband, a priest of Melqart, for his money.",
        "She does not stay to argue. She gathers supporters, loads her dead husband's treasure onto ships, and sails west. On the way she reportedly throws sacks overboard, letting her brother's agents believe the gold is gone.",
        "It is a founding story about a refugee rather than a conqueror — which, for a city built on shipping, fits rather well."],
      key: "Carthage's founding legend has Elissa fleeing Tyre after her brother murdered her husband." },
    { title: "As much land as an ox-hide will cover",
      text: ["She lands on the North African coast and asks the local ruler for land. He offers as much as a single ox-hide can cover, which is meant as a refusal dressed up as generosity.",
        "So she cuts the hide into strips thin as thread, ties them end to end, and lays the line around an entire hill.",
        "The hill became the citadel of Carthage, and the Greeks called it **Byrsa**, which sounds like their word for ox-hide. That is almost certainly the story working backwards from the name rather than the other way round — the Phoenician word for citadel is a better explanation.",
        "Either way, the city's own legend makes its founder win by being cleverer about a contract than the man offering it. Carthage knew what it was."],
      key: "The ox-hide bargain is a Greek pun on Byrsa, the citadel's name, rather than a memory of a real transaction." },
    { title: "What Rome did with her",
      text: ["Centuries later **Virgil** takes Dido and does something ruthless with her. In the *Aeneid* she falls in love with Aeneas, the Trojan refugee who will father the Roman line. He leaves, because duty. She kills herself on a pyre and curses his descendants, calling for an avenger to rise from her bones.",
        "That avenger is Hannibal, and every Roman reader knew it.",
        "So Rome's national poem explains the Punic Wars as a woman's dying curse over a love affair — which conveniently makes a hundred years of Roman aggression into fate rather than policy.",
        "There is no evidence Elissa existed. There is excellent evidence that Rome found her useful."],
      key: "Virgil reworked Dido into an abandoned lover whose curse explains the Punic Wars as destiny rather than Roman policy." },
    { title: "What the ground says",
      text: ["Archaeology puts the earliest settlement at Carthage somewhere in the **eighth century BC**, which is in the right region as the traditional 814 date without confirming it.",
        "That fits a wider pattern. Tyre and Sidon were planting trading posts along the North African and Iberian coasts through this period, chasing metals and safe anchorages. Carthage is one of those, and it happened to have the best position of any of them.",
        "Classification, then: the founding of a Phoenician colony here in the eighth century is **Established**. Elissa herself is **Traditional / Legendary**. The ox-hide is a pun.",
        "The useful habit is separating those three lines rather than accepting or rejecting the story whole."],
      key: "Eighth-century Phoenician settlement at Carthage is established; Elissa and the ox-hide are legend." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What is the ox-hide story most likely explaining?", options: ["A genuine land contract", "The Greek name Byrsa, which sounds like their word for ox-hide", "A Phoenician legal custom", "A Roman joke about Carthaginian greed"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The story appears to run backwards from the name. Phoenician has a better explanation for Byrsa: citadel." },
    { q: "Why did Virgil give Dido a dying curse?", options: ["It was Carthaginian tradition", "It makes the Punic Wars destiny rather than Roman policy", "To explain the founding of Carthage", "It came from Polybius"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Every Roman reader knew the avenger from her bones was Hannibal. It is a hundred years of aggression recast as fate." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "carth-place", set: "carthage", act: "Foundation", title: "The City and Its Neighbours", era: "c. 800 – 264 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "Put your finger on a map where Carthage sat and you have most of its foreign policy already.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The best address in the Mediterranean",
      text: ["The Mediterranean has a waist. Between Cape Bon in Tunisia and the western tip of Sicily the sea narrows to about ninety miles, with a shallow bank running most of the way across.",
        "Everything sailing between the eastern and western halves of that sea has to pass through the gap. Carthage sat on the southern side of it, on a promontory with sheltered water on two sides.",
        "That is the whole strategic story in one sentence: whoever holds this can see, tax, escort or stop the traffic of the entire Mediterranean."],
      key: "Carthage controlled the Mediterranean's narrowest channel, between Cape Bon and Sicily." },
    { title: "The harbour you could not see into",
      text: ["They built to match. The **cothon** was two artificial basins, dug rather than found. The outer one was rectangular, commercial, busy and visible. Behind it, through a channel you could close, was a circular naval harbour with an island in the middle, ringed by covered ship-sheds — somewhere near two hundred and twenty berths, with the admiral's building on the island.",
        "The clever part is what you could not see. From the merchant harbour you had no idea how many warships were in there or how ready they were. Appian says a screen wall stopped traders looking in.",
        "Carthage could be arming for war or empty, and you found out when the ships came out. Both basins are still traceable from the air."],
      key: "The cothon hid Carthage's naval strength behind a screen wall, so no visitor could count its warships." },
    { title: "Who else was in the neighbourhood",
      text: ["Immediately inland were the **Libyans**, farming the best land in North Africa and taxed savagely for it — up to half the harvest in wartime. Remember that figure. It matters enormously later.",
        "West and south were the **Numidian** kingdoms, who produced the finest light cavalry in the western world. Carthage hired them for centuries. Rome eventually hired them instead, and that switch decides a war.",
        "North across the channel was **Sicily**, half Greek and permanently contested. In Italy the **Etruscans** were allies against Greek expansion — and behind them, unremarked for a long time, was a small inland town called Rome."],
      key: "Carthage was ringed by taxed Libyan farmland, Numidian cavalry kingdoms, Greek Sicily and eventually Rome." },
    { title: "A trade policy, not an anecdote",
      text: ["West, past the Pillars of Hercules, was the thing nobody else had: the Atlantic. Tin from Brittany and Cornwall, gold from West Africa, salted fish from the Atlantic coast.",
        "Carthage guarded that route the way a company guards a patent. Strabo reports a Carthaginian captain who deliberately wrecked his own ship on a shoal rather than let a Roman vessel follow him to the tin islands.",
        "The state compensated him for the loss.",
        "That last sentence is the important one. A man ruining himself for the secret is a good story; a state with a standing policy of reimbursing him is a system."],
      key: "Carthage protected its Atlantic trade routes as state policy, compensating a captain who wrecked his ship to shake off a Roman follower." },
    { title: "How it was actually run",
      text: ["Carthage was a republic, and a good one. **Aristotle** puts it in his *Politics* alongside Sparta and Crete as a well-governed state — a remarkable thing for a Greek to say about a non-Greek city, and he says it while the two are fighting over Sicily.",
        "Two chief magistrates called **suffetes** were elected annually. There was a senate of the wealthy, a council of 104 that audited generals, and popular assemblies with real powers when the senate split.",
        "Monarchy had been abandoned early. Power sat with people who owned ships.",
        "The Bible's summary is worth keeping in mind: powerful aristocratic families, elected magistrates and councils all matter. It is not a simple monarchy and it never was."],
      key: "Carthage was a republic of annually elected suffetes, a merchant senate and a council of 104 that audited its generals." },
    { title: "The flaw in the system",
      text: ["Carthaginian citizens did not, as a rule, fight. The army was **hired**: Libyan levies, Numidian horse, Iberian and Gallic infantry, Balearic slingers, Greek officers.",
        "In fairness this is sensible for a trading state. Your citizens are worth more making money than dying in Sicily, and mercenaries are only expensive while you are using them.",
        "But it has two failure modes, and Carthage found both. A hired army cannot absorb a catastrophe — lose it and you must buy another, and the market may not have one. And an unpaid hired army does not go home quietly.",
        "Rome's system was the mirror image: citizens with farms, slower and less specialised, and vastly harder to destroy permanently."],
      key: "Carthage fought with hired troops, which could not absorb a catastrophe and would not go home unpaid." },
    { title: "The Tophet",
      text: ["There is a walled precinct at Carthage holding thousands of small urns. Each contains the cremated remains of an infant or a young animal, and each is marked with a stone. It is called the **Tophet**, and it is the most argued-about site in Punic archaeology.",
        "Greek and Roman sources are lurid and unanimous: Carthaginians burned their own children to Baal Hammon, in numbers, especially when frightened.",
        "So — did they? Genuinely **Contested**. One side reads the urns as exactly that. The other reads them as a cemetery for infants who died naturally, which in the ancient world would be a great many, buried in a sacred precinct with an offering. Both camps point at the same bones."],
      key: "Child sacrifice at the Tophet is genuinely contested; the same bones support an infant cemetery reading." },
    { title: "A warning about your sources",
      text: ["Hold on to the deeper problem, because it applies to everything else in this Set.",
        "Punic literature is essentially gone. Carthage had libraries; after 146 BC the Romans handed them to their Numidian allies and they vanished. What survives about Carthage was written by Greeks and Romans who fought it — and Rome had spent a century arguing that these people were a species of monster who could not be lived alongside.",
        "That is not an occasional bias you can spot and correct for. It shapes the entire surviving picture.",
        "Every claim in this Set carries a discount, and the ones that most flatter Rome carry the largest."],
      key: "Punic literature is lost, so almost everything known about Carthage was written by its enemies." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What was the cothon?", options: ["A Carthaginian law code", "An artificial double harbour whose naval basin could not be seen into", "The council of elders", "A type of warship"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The screen wall was the point. Nobody could count Carthage's warships from the merchant harbour." },
    { q: "What was the structural weakness of Carthage's military system?", options: ["It had no navy", "It relied on hired troops rather than citizens", "Its generals were elected annually", "It banned cavalry"], correct: 1,
      explain: "A hired army cannot absorb a catastrophe, and an unpaid one does not go home quietly. Carthage found both out." },
    { q: "Why must claims about Carthage carry a discount?", options: ["The sources are undated", "Punic literature is lost, so the record comes from Carthage's enemies", "Carthaginians did not write", "All sources are medieval"], correct: 1,
      explain: "A systematic bias, not an occasional one — and the claims that most flatter Rome carry the largest discount." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "carth-sicily", set: "carthage", act: "Empire", title: "The Sicilian Wars", era: "580 – 265 BC", minutes: 6,
  intro: "Three hundred years of on-and-off war over one island. Carthage never won it, never lost it, and never let it go.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Why anyone cared about Sicily",
      text: ["Sicily is the biggest island in the Mediterranean and it grows grain in serious quantity, which in the ancient world is close to printing money. It also sits directly across the sea lane Carthage lived on.",
        "The trouble was that it had been settled from both ends. Greek colonists took the east and south from the eighth century BC — Syracuse, Acragas, Gela, Himera. Phoenicians held the west from **Motya**, **Panormus** and **Lilybaeum**, ports that faced Africa and were a day's sail from it.",
        "In the middle were the Sicels and Elymians, who get almost no lines in this story because both of their neighbours wrote more than they did."],
      key: "Sicily's grain and position made it the permanent contest between Carthage and the Greek cities." },
    { title: "A deadlock, not a stalemate",
      text: ["Neither side could accept the other holding the whole island. Neither side could actually take it.",
        "Carthage could always land an army; it could never keep one supplied indefinitely in hostile country. Syracuse could beat a Carthaginian army in the field; it could never chase it across the water and finish the job in Africa.",
        "So the argument ran for three centuries, in bursts of appalling violence separated by treaties that drew a line across the island and then quietly stopped working.",
        "It is the longest-running war in the ancient Mediterranean, and it is almost forgotten — because both of the states fighting it were later destroyed by a third one that wrote the books."],
      key: "Neither Carthage nor Syracuse could conquer Sicily or accept the other holding it, producing three centuries of recurring war." },
    { title: "Himera, and a useful coincidence",
      text: ["The first big Carthaginian disaster came at **Himera** in 480 BC. Herodotus says the commander **Hamilcar** spent the battle at an altar sacrificing, and when he saw his army breaking, threw himself into the fire.",
        "Greek tradition put Himera on the same day as **Salamis**. Consider how useful that is if you are Greek: one afternoon, two ends of the world, Greeks holding off Persia in the east and Carthage in the west, and civilisation surviving by providence.",
        "It is a wonderful story and almost certainly a later construction. The app files it as **Interpretation**. It tells you a great deal about how Greeks wished to see themselves and nothing about what Carthage thought it was doing."],
      key: "The tradition that Himera and Salamis were fought on the same day is a Greek construction, not a verified synchronism." },
    { title: "Agathocles changes the question",
      text: ["Defeat kept Carthage out of Sicily for seventy years. When the war restarted in 409 BC it ran, on and off, for another century and a half — against Dionysius I of Syracuse, who cracked Motya open with catapults, and against Timoleon.",
        "Then **Agathocles**, in 310 BC, shut up in Syracuse under blockade, did the thing nobody expected. He left. He took his army by sea to **Africa** and burned his own ships on the beach so every man understood there was no way back.",
        "He tore up Carthaginian territory for three years and came close to taking the city. He failed, and abandoned his own army to save himself.",
        "But he had proved something: hit Carthage at home and it panics. A Roman would read that and take notes."],
      key: "Agathocles invaded Africa in 310 BC and proved Carthage could be threatened at home." },
    { title: "What the fighting was actually about",
      text: ["Underneath three centuries of siege sat something less dramatic. Carthage was not trying to build a Sicilian empire. It was protecting a monopoly.",
        "The treaties with Rome give it away, and we have them because Polybius saw the bronze originals. The earliest specifies where Roman ships may sail, requires trading in Carthaginian territory to happen in front of a herald, and forbids sailing past a fixed cape at all. A later one bars Romans from Sardinia and Libya entirely.",
        "Read those as diplomacy between equals and they make no sense. Read them as a schedule of permissions issued by whoever controls the water, and they are perfectly clear."],
      key: "Carthage's treaties with Rome were schedules of permission, not agreements between equals." },
    { title: "A lower bar than conquest",
      text: ["What those permissions protected: West African gold, Iberian silver, Sardinian grain, purple dye worth more by weight than silver, and above all the carrying trade — being the ship everyone else's goods travel in.",
        "So Carthage never needed to rule Sicily. It needed nobody else to rule Sicily. That is a much lower bar, and for three hundred years it cleared it.",
        "It became unclearable when a new kind of state arrived. When **Pyrrhus of Epirus** campaigned through Italy and Sicily in the 270s BC and briefly looked like unifying the island, Carthage and Rome fought on the same side — the last time that ever happened.",
        "Pyrrhus, sailing away, is supposed to have looked back and remarked what a fine wrestling ground they were leaving for Rome and Carthage. He was right within a decade."],
      key: "Carthage needed only to keep others out of Sicily — until Rome arrived as a new kind of competitor." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why could neither Carthage nor the Greeks settle the Sicilian question?", options: ["Neither could conquer the whole island, and neither could accept the other holding it", "A treaty forbade fighting", "Rome intervened each time", "The island was too poor to fight over"], correct: 0,
      explain: "Structural deadlock, not lack of effort — which is why the wars recur across three centuries." },
    { q: "How does the app classify the tradition that Himera was fought on the same day as Salamis?", options: ["Established", "Probable", "Interpretation — it reflects Greek self-image", "Unknown"], correct: 2,
      explain: "It serves a Greek story about civilisation defended at both ends of the world. A reading, not a synchronism." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "carth-hanno", set: "carthage", act: "Empire", title: "Hanno the Navigator", era: "c. 500 BC", minutes: 6,
  intro: "A Carthaginian fleet sails off the edge of the Greek map and files a report we can still read. It is very nearly the only Carthaginian voice we have.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Turn left at the Pillars",
      text: ["Around 500 BC a suffete named **Hanno** took a fleet out through the Pillars of Hercules and, instead of turning right for the known world, turned left and went down the Atlantic coast of Africa.",
        "The stated purpose was colonisation. The account claims sixty ships and thirty thousand settlers, which is certainly inflated, though it tells you the scale they wanted imagined.",
        "And here is the remarkable part: we can read his report. The ***Periplus of Hanno*** survives as a Greek translation of a Punic text apparently set up in the temple of Baal at Carthage. It is a handful of pages in flat first-person plural, and close to the only thing a Carthaginian wrote about Carthaginian business that has come down to us."],
      key: "Hanno sailed the Atlantic coast of Africa c. 500 BC; his Periplus survives as a Greek translation of a Punic text." },
    { title: "It gets stranger going south",
      text: ["Coastal lagoons with elephants in them. Crocodiles and hippos in a river. People in animal skins throwing stones at the ships.",
        "Then one night a whole coastline on fire, with drums and pipes and shouting coming off it, which unnerved the crews enough that they kept sailing. Then a mountain pouring flame into the sea, which they named the **Chariot of the Gods**.",
        "And finally an island full of hairy people whom their Libyan interpreters called ***gorillai***. Three were taken, fought so hard they had to be killed, and their skins were carried back to Carthage.",
        "In 1847 an American missionary borrowed that word out of the *Periplus* to name a newly described ape. The animal in your local zoo wears a Punic name written down by a Carthaginian admiral."],
      key: "The Periplus describes volcanoes, drums in the night and 'gorillai' — the source of the modern animal's name." },
    { title: "How far did he actually get?",
      text: ["**Contested**, and the range of serious opinion is embarrassingly wide: southern Morocco at the cautious end, Sierra Leone or even Gabon at the ambitious one.",
        "The argument turns on matching described features — a volcano, a gulf, a river with crocodiles — to real coastline, and the further south you push the more it depends on which volcano you are willing to accept.",
        "Some scholars doubt the voyage as written altogether, reading the text as part log and part advertisement: a state showing off its navy's reach, or making the route sound unusable to anyone else.",
        "The hairy islanders might be chimpanzees, baboons, or a real people described by frightened men through two layers of translation."],
      key: "How far Hanno sailed is contested, and some read the Periplus as partly promotional." },
    { title: "The other direction",
      text: ["Carthage also sent **Himilco** north, up the Atlantic face of Europe toward Brittany and probably Britain, chasing tin. No tin, no bronze — it was worth an ocean voyage.",
        "Fragments of that account survive too, and they describe a sea choked with weed, no wind at all, and monsters.",
        "Which is either a genuine observation of Sargasso-type water or exactly what you would write if you wanted competitors to stay at home. Given what we know about Carthaginian trade policy, I would not rule out the second.",
        "Either way, this is a state systematically exploring ocean coastline with public funding and written records, while Greek geography was still hugging the Mediterranean shore."],
      key: "Himilco sailed north for tin; Carthage ran state-funded ocean exploration in both directions." },
    { title: "An empire of harbours",
      text: ["This is the thing most easily missed about Carthage, because it is the opposite of how Rome worked.",
        "Carthage did not expand by conquest. It planted a chain of coastal footholds: a sheltered anchorage, a warehouse, a temple, a market, and a working arrangement with whoever ran the interior. It wanted the harbour, not the hinterland.",
        "That is cheap, it scales fast, it needs very few of your own citizens — and it leaves almost nothing behind. No roads, no grid-planned colonies, no monumental inscriptions announcing the conquest of anybody.",
        "Which is a large part of why Carthage is archaeologically so much thinner than its importance deserves."],
      key: "Carthage expanded through chains of coastal trading footholds rather than territorial conquest." },
    { title: "The weakness inside the strength",
      text: ["By the mid-third century BC the network ran from the Atlantic coast of Morocco through southern Iberia, the Balearics, Sardinia and western Sicily. On paper Carthage was the dominant power of the western Mediterranean, richer than any Greek state, with a navy nobody could match.",
        "Its weakness was its strength inside out. An empire of harbours has no depth. There is no interior to fall back into.",
        "It can be unpicked one port at a time by anyone patient enough to spend the years, and it depends absolutely on controlling the water between the links.",
        "In 264 BC Rome got involved in a squabble at Messana about a garrison, and began — without the faintest intention of doing so — doing precisely that."],
      key: "An empire of harbours has no depth and can be unpicked one port at a time." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What is the Periplus of Hanno?", options: ["A Roman forgery", "A Greek translation of a Punic account of an Atlantic voyage", "A Carthaginian law code", "A Greek epic poem"], correct: 1,
      explain: "One of very few Punic texts to survive in any form, and only because it passed through Greek." },
    { q: "What characterised the Punic colonial model?", options: ["Mass settlement of farmers inland", "Chains of coastal trading footholds with local arrangements", "Direct military occupation", "Religious conversion of neighbours"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Harbours and warehouses rather than provinces — cheap, flexible, and archaeologically almost invisible." },
  ],
},
);

/* Beats marked BIBLE are lifted from the Content Bible §5 as written.
   Beats without the marker cover ground the Bible does not reach. */
CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "carth-after-first", set: "carthage", act: "Decline", title: "What the First War Cost", era: "264 – 237 BC", minutes: 5,
  intro: "The war itself lives in the Wars tab. This chapter is the bill that arrived afterwards — because the bill is what causes the next war.",
  beats: [
    /* BIBLE · CAR-02 */
    { title: "The First Punic War begins",
      text: ["The immediate crisis centres on Messana in Sicily and the Mamertines who control it. Appeals for outside help draw both Carthage and Rome into the dispute.",
        "What begins as a local intervention becomes a war between the dominant naval power of the western Mediterranean and an Italian land power that has barely begun operating overseas.",
        "Rome's problem is obvious: Sicily cannot be won by pretending the sea is not there."],
      key: "264 BC: a local dispute at Messana pulls both powers into a war neither planned." },
    /* BIBLE · CAR-03 */
    { title: "Rome learns to fight at sea",
      text: ["Ancient tradition says the Romans used a captured or grounded Carthaginian quinquereme as a model while rapidly building a fleet. The story may simplify a more complicated process, but Rome unquestionably develops major naval capacity with remarkable speed.",
        "The famous corvus boarding device allows Roman infantry to turn some naval encounters into close combat. At Mylae in 260 BC, Rome wins its first major naval victory.",
        "Carthage still has deeper maritime experience. Rome has demonstrated something that will become one of its defining strengths: when an enemy presents a problem, Rome can copy, adapt and keep trying after expensive failures."],
      key: "260 BC: Mylae. Rome's answer to a naval problem is to build a navy and keep rebuilding it." },
    { title: "The bill",
      text: ["Three thousand two hundred talents over ten years, on top of the cost of building and losing fleet after fleet. And Sicily went — its western half, held for something like four hundred years, became Rome's first overseas province, and was promptly taxed to help pay for the next war.",
        "Now think what that does to a state built the way Carthage was. Its entire military rested on hiring soldiers, and hiring soldiers rests on revenue.",
        "Carthage had just lost a large chunk of the revenue and taken on the debt in the same document."],
      key: "Carthage lost Sicily and took on a 3,200-talent indemnity in the same treaty." },
    { title: "A state defeated, a general who was not",
      text: ["The political damage was worse than the money, and it decides everything afterwards.",
        "**Hamilcar Barca** had held the mountains of western Sicily for years. He had never been beaten in the field. He was still holding when the government negotiated a peace over his head and instructed him to arrange the evacuation.",
        "He obeyed. He also resigned rather than hand over the weapons himself.",
        "So the war produced a state that had been defeated and a general who had not. Those two things do not live comfortably in one country."],
      key: "Hamilcar came home undefeated to a peace signed over his head, and resigned rather than surrender the arms." },
    /* BIBLE · CAR-04 */
    { title: "Defeat, debt and what came next",
      text: ["The First Punic War ends in 241 BC after Roman victory near the Aegates Islands. Carthage gives up Sicily and accepts a large indemnity.",
        "Then unpaid or underpaid troops revolt in the brutal Mercenary War, sometimes called the Truceless War. Carthage survives, but Rome exploits its weakness to take Sardinia and imposes further financial pressure.",
        "For the Barcid family, this matters. Carthage has lost territory, money and prestige. Hamilcar Barca now looks toward Iberia."],
      key: "241 BC: Carthage loses Sicily, then nearly loses itself to its own unpaid army." },
    { title: "Sardinia, and a hostile witness",
      text: ["The Sardinia seizure deserves a moment, because of who complains about it.",
        "**Polybius** is Greek, wrote inside the household of the Roman general who destroyed Carthage, and is nowhere sentimental about Carthaginians. On this he says flatly that the seizure was contrary to all justice, that Rome had no reasonable ground whatever, and that it was among the principal causes of the war that followed.",
        "When a source that partial concedes his own side was in the wrong, the concession is worth more than any hostile witness's accusation.",
        "Keep that as a habit. It is one of the most useful tools you have for reading ancient history."],
      key: "Polybius, a pro-Roman source, calls the seizure of Sardinia unjust and a cause of the next war." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What did Rome demonstrate during the First Punic War?", options: ["Superior seamanship from the start", "That it could copy, adapt and keep rebuilding after expensive failures", "That it preferred diplomacy", "That Carthage had no navy"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It lost fleet after fleet and built another each time. That capacity, not skill at sea, is what won it." },
    { q: "What does Polybius say about Rome's seizure of Sardinia?", options: ["That it was lawful under treaty", "That it was contrary to justice and a cause of the next war", "That it never happened", "That Carthage requested it"], correct: 1,
      explain: "A pro-Roman source conceding Roman fault is unusually strong evidence." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "carth-truceless", set: "carthage", act: "Decline", title: "The Truceless War", era: "241 – 237 BC", minutes: 6,
  intro: "The most savage war Carthage ever fought was not against Rome. It was against its own army, and it nearly finished the city.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Unpaid",
      text: ["The army came home from Sicily in 241 BC expecting years of back pay. Carthage was broke, and made a decision that looks reasonable on paper: instead of paying, it moved the troops out of the city to a holding camp, kept them together, and opened negotiations to settle the debt at a discount.",
        "There were around twenty thousand of them, and the only thing they had in common was that Carthage owed them money. Otherwise they were Libyans, Iberians, Gauls, Ligurians, Greeks and Balearic islanders with no shared language.",
        "Polybius notes that officers could not address the whole force in any one tongue, so every message went through interpreters who could be suspected of shading it. And were."],
      key: "In 241 BC Carthage kept its unpaid mercenary army camped together while haggling over the debt." },
    { title: "It stops being about pay",
      text: ["Picture it: twenty thousand armed, unpaid, bored professional soldiers, camped together, haggled with in a language most cannot follow, by people they already suspect.",
        "They mutinied under **Spendius**, an escaped Campanian slave who understood that any settlement put him back on a cross, and **Mathos**, a Libyan who knew the same was coming for his own people.",
        "Then the revolt spread into the **Libyan** countryside, where Carthage had been taking half the harvest to fund the war and the resentment was three generations deep.",
        "Carthage suddenly faced a veteran army of perhaps seventy thousand in its own back garden, with almost no citizen soldiers — having spent four centuries arranging never to need any."],
      key: "The mutiny spread into the heavily taxed Libyan countryside and became a war for Carthage's survival." },
    { title: "Why 'truceless'",
      text: ["What makes this war different is that both sides deliberately destroyed the possibility of ending it.",
        "The rebel leadership had a problem: their men could always be bought back, and Carthage was rich. Spendius solved it by arranging the murder of the hostages — the general **Gisco** and some seven hundred Carthaginians — and announcing that any future envoy would get the same.",
        "That was not rage. That was policy, and it is chillingly effective. Afterwards no man in the rebel army could open negotiations, because there was nothing left for Carthage to forgive.",
        "Carthage replied in kind, and took no captives thereafter."],
      key: "Both sides destroyed the possibility of negotiation deliberately; Polybius called it the most savage war he knew." },
    { title: "The conclusion Hamilcar drew",
      text: ["Carthage turned back to the man it had sidelined. **Hamilcar Barca** won the war by 237 BC, and won it by manoeuvre against men who had served under him and knew exactly how he worked.",
        "Then he sat down and worked out what had gone wrong, and his conclusions were structural. Carthage's government had lost a war it could have won, signed a peace over its general's head, failed to pay the army that peace released, and nearly been destroyed by the consequences.",
        "Any army belonging to that government would be underpaid at the worst possible moment, forever.",
        "So the next army would not belong to it. He looked west at **Iberia**: silver, manpower, and two thousand kilometres between himself and the Carthaginian senate."],
      key: "Hamilcar concluded that any future army must be funded and commanded independently of Carthage's government." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What caused the Truceless War?", options: ["A religious dispute", "Carthage delaying payment to its returning mercenary army", "A Roman invasion", "A Numidian succession crisis"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The mercenary system's basic failure mode — an army with no citizenship stake, and a state that could not pay it." },
    { q: "Why did Spendius have the hostages killed?", options: ["Revenge for a defeat", "To make negotiation impossible, so his men could not be bought back", "On Mathos's orders", "To provoke Rome"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Policy, not rage. Afterwards there was nothing left for Carthage to forgive." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "carth-spain", set: "carthage", act: "Decline", title: "The Conquest of Spain", era: "237 – 218 BC", minutes: 6,
  intro: "A beaten state goes out and builds a better empire than the one it lost — then hands it to a family instead of a government.",
  beats: [
    /* BIBLE · CAR-05 */
    { title: "The Barcids build in Iberia",
      text: ["Hamilcar Barca leads Carthaginian expansion in Iberia, creating a new base of manpower, resources and political power. After his death, Hasdrubal the Fair continues the project and founds New Carthage.",
        "Ancient tradition says the young Hannibal swore never to be a friend of Rome. It is a famous story, not something we can verify as a literal childhood oath.",
        "When Hasdrubal is assassinated in 221 BC, Hannibal — still only in his mid-twenties — is chosen as commander."],
      key: "237–221 BC: the Barcids build a second Carthaginian empire in Iberia, and Hannibal inherits it at twenty-five." },
    { title: "Underneath all of it: silver",
      text: ["The mines of the Sierra Morena and the hills behind modern Cartagena were among the richest deposits in the ancient world.",
        "Polybius went and looked at them, and describes forty thousand men working the shafts near New Carthage and a daily yield to the treasury of twenty-five thousand drachmas. Slaves cut the galleries, water-screws drained them, and the metal left through the harbour in ingots.",
        "This is a money-printing operation with an army attached. In nine years a bankrupt state had rebuilt its finances, its army and its fleet — through a project that belonged, in every practical sense, to one family."],
      key: "Iberian silver rebuilt Carthaginian finances, under Barcid rather than state control." },
    { title: "A capital four hundred miles from the council",
      text: ["In 227 BC Hasdrubal founded **Qart Hadasht** — New City, the same name Carthage itself carried, which tells you how modest the ambition was. Romans called it Carthago Nova; today it is Cartagena.",
        "Into it went a palace, a mint, arsenals, dockyards and the whole administration of the silver. A capital, four hundred miles by sea from the council supposedly running it.",
        "The coins make the point more quietly. Barcid silver struck in Spain carries a male head dressed as the god Melqart, and the face changes as the commanders change.",
        "Carthage had never put a living man on its money."],
      key: "New Carthage was a Barcid capital with its own mint, and Barcid coins carried living commanders' faces." },
    /* BIBLE · CAR-06 */
    { title: "Saguntum and the road to war",
      text: ["Hannibal attacks Saguntum, a city with ties to Rome. After a long siege the city falls. Rome demands satisfaction from Carthage; Carthage refuses.",
        "A useful correction matters here: Saguntum lay south of the Ebro. The outbreak of war cannot simply be reduced to 'Hannibal crossed the treaty line and attacked it'. The diplomacy and treaty obligations are more complicated.",
        "In 218 BC the Second Punic War begins. Hannibal chooses not to wait for Rome to attack Carthage. He takes the war toward Italy."],
      key: "219 BC: Saguntum falls. It lay south of the Ebro, so the treaty argument is not as simple as it is usually told." },
    { title: "Who was responsible",
      text: ["**Contested**, and contested by the ancient writers themselves.",
        "Polybius names three causes: Hamilcar's unextinguished anger, the Barcid success in Spain that made a second war thinkable at all, and Rome's seizure of Sardinia — which, remember, he himself calls contrary to all justice.",
        "He blames Barcid ambition and concedes the Carthaginian grievance in the same argument.",
        "Both can be true at once. That is usually the answer with war-guilt questions, and the treaty had been drafted loosely enough for each side to be genuinely convinced it was inside the lines."],
      key: "Polybius blames Barcid ambition and concedes Rome's injustice over Sardinia in the same argument." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What funded the Barcid recovery in Spain?", options: ["Roman subsidies", "Iberian silver mines", "Egyptian grain", "Sicilian tribute"], correct: 1,
      explain: "A state-scale revenue stream controlled by a single family." },
    { q: "Why is the Saguntum treaty argument not simple?", options: ["No treaty existed", "Saguntum lay south of the Ebro, inside the zone Rome had conceded", "Rome had already declared war", "Hannibal never attacked it"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It cannot be reduced to Hannibal crossing a line. The diplomacy is more complicated than the usual telling." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "carth-italy", set: "carthage", act: "Decline", title: "Hannibal in Italy", era: "218 – 206 BC", minutes: 4,
  intro: "Sixteen years in Italy, three annihilating victories, and a war he still could not finish. The battles themselves are in the Wars tab; this is the strategic shape of them.",
  beats: [
    /* BIBLE · CAR-07 */
    { title: "Hannibal crosses the Alps",
      text: ["Hannibal moves from Iberia through Gaul and across the Alps into Italy. The crossing is costly through fighting, desertion, terrain and weather, and the exact route remains debated.",
        "The strategic achievement is more important than the tourist question of which pass he used. Rome expected to fight for Spain and perhaps Africa. Hannibal has brought a major army into northern Italy.",
        "Gaulish recruits help rebuild his strength. Rome now has a brilliant enemy operating on Italian soil."],
      key: "218 BC: the strategic point of the Alps crossing is where the war is now fought, not which pass he used." },
    /* BIBLE · CAR-08 */
    { title: "Three Roman disasters",
      text: ["Trebia in 218. Lake Trasimene in 217. Cannae in 216.",
        "At Trebia, Hannibal manipulates an eager Roman commander, cold troops and a hidden force under Mago. At Trasimene he turns terrain and poor visibility into a gigantic ambush. At Cannae he allows his centre to bend while his stronger infantry and cavalry create the most famous double envelopment in ancient warfare.",
        "Cannae is catastrophic. But Rome does something Hannibal cannot force it to stop doing: Rome refuses to surrender."],
      key: "218 Trebia → 217 Trasimene → 216 Cannae." },
    /* BIBLE · CAR-09 */
    { title: "Why Cannae does not end the war",
      text: ["After Cannae, some Roman allies defect, most famously Capua. But the alliance system does not collapse completely. Rome raises more armies, avoids offering Hannibal another ideal decisive battle and continues fighting in multiple theatres.",
        "Hannibal can defeat Roman armies. Destroying Rome's political and manpower system is harder. He also lacks an easy way to storm the heavily defended city of Rome itself.",
        "The war becomes a contest of endurance — exactly the sort of war Rome is unusually good at surviving."],
      key: "Winning battles and destroying Rome's manpower system are different problems, and Hannibal could only do the first." },
    /* BIBLE · CAR-10 */
    { title: "Spain starts slipping away",
      text: ["A young Publius Cornelius Scipio takes command in Iberia. In 209 BC he captures New Carthage, the Barcid strategic base, using a coordinated assault that includes troops crossing a shallow lagoon when conditions permit.",
        "In 207 BC Hannibal's brother Hasdrubal reaches Italy with reinforcements, but Roman forces intercept him at the Metaurus. Hasdrubal is killed.",
        "The possibility of the two Barcid armies combining in Italy disappears. Hannibal remains dangerous, but the strategic direction of the war is changing."],
      key: "209 BC New Carthage falls; 207 BC the Metaurus ends any chance of the Barcid armies uniting." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why did Cannae not end the war?", options: ["Hannibal was wounded", "Rome's alliance system, manpower and refusal to negotiate survived the defeat", "Carthage recalled him", "The Romans won it"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Defeating Roman armies and destroying Rome's political system are different problems." },
    { q: "What changed the strategic direction of the war?", options: ["Cannae", "The loss of New Carthage in 209 and Hasdrubal's death at the Metaurus in 207", "The crossing of the Alps", "Capua's defection"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Together they ended any prospect of the two Barcid armies combining in Italy." },
  ],
},
);

CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "carth-zama", set: "carthage", act: "Fall", title: "No Longer an Empire", era: "202 – 151 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "Carthage survived the Second Punic War. What it did not survive was the peace terms — and the clause that killed it looks like the least dramatic one in the document.",
  beats: [
    /* BIBLE · CAR-11 */
    { title: "Zama",
      text: ["Scipio carries the war into Africa and allies with the Numidian king Masinissa. Carthage recalls Hannibal from Italy.",
        "At Zama in 202 BC, Hannibal reportedly fields a large elephant force. Scipio arranges his infantry so lanes can open through the Roman formation, reducing the elephants' ability to smash straight through it. Roman and Numidian cavalry leave the field in pursuit and later return against Hannibal's rear.",
        "Scipio wins. The Second Punic War is effectively decided. The formal peace follows in 201 BC.",
        "Remember 202 for another reason: in China, Liu Bang establishes the Han dynasty in the same year."],
      key: "202 BC = Zama + the beginning of Han." },
    { title: "The terms",
      text: ["Carthage kept its African territory and lost everything else — Spain, the islands, every overseas possession. It handed over its elephants and promised to train no more.",
        "It surrendered its navy down to ten triremes. Livy describes the remaining five hundred ships towed out past the harbour mouth and burned in full view of the city, and says the people watching from the walls mourned as though Carthage itself were alight.",
        "It accepted an indemnity of ten thousand talents over fifty years.",
        "Notice what Rome had not done. It annexed nothing. It left a wealthy trading city standing with its constitution, territory and commerce intact."],
      key: "The 202 BC terms stripped Carthage of empire and navy while leaving the city and its wealth in place." },
    { title: "The clause that mattered",
      text: ["And then one clause that mattered more than all the rest together. **Carthage would make no war outside Africa at all, and none inside Africa without Rome's permission.**",
        "A state that cannot go to war without another state's consent is a client, whatever the paperwork says.",
        "That clause was not a humiliation to be lived down over time. It was a mechanism, and it took fifty years to finish running.",
        "When the terms were read at home, a senator rose to argue against them and Hannibal physically pulled him off the platform — then told the assembly to accept, and to be grateful the terms were no worse."],
      key: "Carthage could wage no war without Rome's permission, which made it a client state in all but name." },
    /* BIBLE · CAR-13 */
    { title: "Masinissa keeps taking",
      text: ["Masinissa, Rome's Numidian ally from the Second Punic War, repeatedly expands at Carthage's expense. Carthage appeals to Rome, but the disputes rarely produce the protection Carthage wants.",
        "The treaty creates a trap: Carthage is expected not to fight without Roman approval, while a Roman ally steadily pressures its territory.",
        "Eventually Carthage fights Numidia without permission. Rome now has the treaty violation it needs."],
      key: "The permission clause turned a border dispute into a legal trap with only one exit." },
    { title: "Why the trap was airtight",
      text: ["Sit with the position, because there is no move in it.",
        "Carthage could not fight, because fighting without permission broke the treaty. It could not win an arbitration, because the arbitrator had installed the other party. It could not appeal past Rome, because there was nobody else left to appeal to.",
        "Resisting meant war with Rome. Not resisting meant losing the country in instalments.",
        "Every year Carthage complied was a year it got smaller, and the only door out led straight to the end."],
      key: "Carthage could neither fight, nor win arbitration, nor appeal — so compliance shrank it annually." },
    /* BIBLE · CAR-12 */
    { title: "Carthage without an empire",
      text: ["Carthage survives but loses its overseas empire, much of its military freedom and its great-power position. It must pay Rome and cannot wage war without Roman permission.",
        "That does not mean the city becomes poor. Its economy recovers impressively, which eventually alarms some Romans.",
        "Hannibal himself enters Carthaginian politics and becomes suffete around 196 BC. He attacks corruption and reforms finances, helping Carthage meet its obligations. Political enemies accuse him of plotting with Antiochus III; under Roman pressure he flees in 195 BC and never returns."],
      key: "Carthage recovered economically under a treaty that removed its capacity to defend itself." },
    { title: "The part nobody tells you",
      text: ["Hannibal's second career deserves more than a clause. He did not flee after Zama or retire onto his estates. Six years later he stood for office, was elected suffete, and turned on his own governing class.",
        "The Council of 104 had held office for life and answered to nobody. He made it annually elected.",
        "Then he opened the accounts, and this is the killer. State revenues were more than enough to pay Rome's indemnity without a single new tax. The money was simply being stolen, in quantity, by the men complaining loudest about the burden.",
        "Within a few years Carthage offered to clear the whole remaining indemnity in one payment. Rome refused. A Carthage out of debt is a Carthage out of reach."],
      key: "Hannibal as suffete showed the indemnity was affordable and being embezzled; Rome then refused early payment." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Which peace term mattered most in the long run?", options: ["The loss of elephants", "The ban on waging war without Rome's permission", "The loss of Sicily", "The surrender of hostages"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It made self-defence against Masinissa legally impossible — the mechanism that produced the Third Punic War." },
    { q: "Why did Rome refuse Carthage's offer to pay the indemnity early?", options: ["The sum was too small", "A debt-free Carthage would be an independent one", "Rome had lost the treaty", "Carthage lacked the money"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The indemnity was leverage as much as compensation." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "carth-end", set: "carthage", act: "Fall", title: "Delenda Est", era: "149 – 146 BC", minutes: 6,
  intro: "Carthage recovered commercially, complied legally, and kept every clause of the treaty. It was destroyed anyway.",
  beats: [
    /* BIBLE · CAR-14 */
    { title: "Cato wants Carthage gone",
      text: ["Roman politician Cato the Elder becomes famous for insisting that Carthage must be destroyed. The exact formula usually quoted — 'Carthago delenda est' — is a later conventional wording, but his advocacy is real.",
        "Not every Roman agrees. Scipio Nasica is associated with the opposing idea that a dangerous rival helps keep Rome disciplined.",
        "In 149 BC Rome chooses war. The Third Punic War begins."],
      key: "The phrase is later convention; the campaign to destroy Carthage is established fact." },
    { title: "The fig, and the argument Nasica lost",
      text: ["Cato had been on an embassy to arbitrate one of Masinissa's encroachments, and came home unsettled by a full harbour and a countryside of orchards.",
        "Pliny says he came into the Senate, shook a fresh African fig out of a fold of his toga onto the floor, and asked how far away the country was that grew it. Three days' sail, he told them.",
        "It is a magnificent piece of political theatre — the fig is still fresh, therefore the enemy is still close — and it worked.",
        "Nasica's counter-argument deserves more attention than it gets: remove the rival and Roman aggression turns inward. He lost. Within two generations Rome was fighting itself."],
      key: "Cato's fresh fig argued that a rich Carthage was three days away; Nasica's warning about Rome turning inward lost." },
    /* BIBLE · CAR-15 */
    { title: "Rome's demands become impossible",
      text: ["Carthage initially tries to avoid destruction. It provides hostages. It surrenders weapons.",
        "Then Rome demands that the Carthaginians abandon their city and resettle inland.",
        "At that point surrender no longer means accepting Roman dominance. It means accepting the destruction of Carthage as Carthage.",
        "The city chooses resistance and turns itself into a weapons factory. The final war will last roughly three years."],
      key: "149 BC: after the hostages and the weapons, Rome demanded the city itself be abandoned." },
    { title: "The order of the demands",
      text: ["Watch the sequence, because the sequence is the whole thing.",
        "First three hundred hostages, children of noble families. Carthage delivered them. Next all weapons and war material — a reported two hundred thousand sets of armour and two thousand catapults. The consuls praised the city for its obedience.",
        "Only then, with the hostages gone and the arsenals empty, came the demand to move ten miles inland.",
        "Rome took the compliance first and made the impossible demand last. That is the order you use when what you want is the refusal, not the surrender."],
      key: "Rome extracted hostages and weapons before making the one demand that could not be met." },
    /* BIBLE · CAR-16 */
    { title: "The end of Carthage",
      text: ["Scipio Aemilianus eventually breaks into the city. Fighting continues through streets, buildings and rooftops for days before the final defenders retreat to the Byrsa.",
        "Large numbers of survivors are enslaved. Ancient figures should be treated cautiously, but the destruction and enslavement are not myths.",
        "The famous story that Romans salted the earth is not ancient history; it is a much later legend.",
        "Polybius, who knew Scipio, preserves the image of the Roman commander reflecting on the destruction and the possibility that Rome might one day suffer the same fate.",
        "In the same year, Roman forces also destroy Corinth in Greece."],
      key: "146 BC: Carthage and Corinth fall. Rome has almost no peer left in the Mediterranean." },
    { title: "What the city did with three years",
      text: ["The detail worth keeping from the siege is what a disarmed city managed.",
        "Public buildings were stripped for metal and temples for timber. The accounts claim a daily output of a hundred and forty shields, three hundred swords, five hundred spears and a thousand catapult bolts, with women cutting off their hair to make cordage for torsion springs.",
        "Carthage had handed over two hundred thousand sets of armour and then held out for three years.",
        "A century later Rome founded a colony on the same ground. It became one of the great cities of the empire and, in time, the see of Augustine. The place Rome erased is the place Rome rebuilt."],
      key: "A disarmed Carthage rebuilt an arsenal from its own temples and held out three years." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What was Rome's final demand before the war?", options: ["A larger indemnity", "The surrender of Hannibal", "That Carthage abandon its coastal city and move inland", "Conversion to Roman religion"], correct: 2,
      explain: "Framed as relocation, and functioning as an extinction order for a maritime trading society." },
    { q: "How should 'Rome salted the fields of Carthage' be classified?", options: ["Established", "Probable", "A much later legend, not ancient history", "Traditional from Polybius"], correct: 2,
      explain: "No ancient writer mentions it. The story enters the record far later." },
  ],
},
);
